WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.640
One idea they're going to be debating here in the European Parliament next month is this.

00:04.640 --> 00:09.040
If job-destroying robots are replacing tax-paying humans,

00:09.040 --> 00:11.760
well, perhaps we need to tax the robots.

00:12.880 --> 00:17.840
My proposal is that we monitor exactly what is happening on the job market,

00:17.840 --> 00:20.000
on the employment sector.

00:20.000 --> 00:24.480
And if robots are taking over more jobs than new jobs are created,

00:24.480 --> 00:27.840
we will be, or most of member states and of the governance,

00:27.840 --> 00:31.520
we'll be in a difficult position to collect enough money

00:31.520 --> 00:35.360
to finance all the services that we need.

00:35.360 --> 00:39.760
So if this is the case, I think we should think on taxing robots

00:40.640 --> 00:45.600
because someone has to pay for our normal life,

00:45.600 --> 00:50.080
for the infrastructures, for the services that the government should provide to the people.

00:52.480 --> 00:53.920
But how simple would this be?

00:54.800 --> 00:57.120
Some robots are easy to identify.

00:57.120 --> 01:00.720
The company replacing workers with a robot like Baxter here

01:00.720 --> 01:03.360
would be relatively straightforward to tax.

01:03.360 --> 01:07.280
But this wave of innovation is not all about the physical world.

01:15.520 --> 01:19.200
Much of this revolution, though, is taking place out of sight,

01:19.200 --> 01:24.640
inside reassuringly familiar-looking computer cases and server racks.

01:24.640 --> 01:28.400
But these new machines are learning and thinking.

01:28.400 --> 01:32.880
And learning and thinking is currently how much of humanity earns its living.

01:36.480 --> 01:37.760
Welcome to SMAC.

01:38.800 --> 01:40.800
It stands for smart accountancy.

01:41.360 --> 01:45.440
This Berlin-based start-up has harnessed artificial intelligence

01:45.440 --> 01:49.280
in an attempt to revolutionise the boring old accountancy profession.

01:51.280 --> 01:53.120
The process looks familiar enough,

01:53.120 --> 01:57.360
clients of the service will scan their invoices and receipts into a computer.

01:57.360 --> 02:01.200
But inside, the software is doing something extraordinary.

02:01.920 --> 02:04.800
Instead of human accountants looking at the documents,

02:04.800 --> 02:09.440
the computer is figuring out the figures, adding them to the correct accounts.

02:10.160 --> 02:12.960
Text on paper is pretty easy,

02:12.960 --> 02:15.440
but then you have to interpret the text,

02:15.440 --> 02:21.920
and the system has to know what is that number here and what is that number here,

02:21.920 --> 02:25.360
and where is the text information on the receipt.

02:25.360 --> 02:28.320
And that's interpreting the information,

02:28.320 --> 02:33.680
and that's really, in many, many areas, a revolution that we are seeing.

02:33.680 --> 02:36.160
For example, self-driving cars.

02:36.720 --> 02:40.080
Is that a person crossing the street,

02:40.080 --> 02:43.760
or is it just water on the street mirroring something?

02:43.760 --> 02:45.920
Can I go on driving, or do I have to stop?

02:45.920 --> 02:48.800
That's understanding, interpretation work.

02:48.800 --> 02:53.040
And that's also the work that has to be done here for the accounting purposes.

02:53.040 --> 02:55.360
The system really needs to do the interpretation.

02:56.800 --> 02:59.360
At SMAC, they say this will lead to less mundane,

02:59.360 --> 03:02.000
more interesting work for human accountants.

03:02.000 --> 03:05.680
But again, the question is, how many of these human jobs will survive?

03:06.560 --> 03:10.240
We are progressing very, very quickly in terms of innovation,

03:10.240 --> 03:14.640
but we have no idea where we are heading and where we want to reach.

03:15.200 --> 03:20.240
And I feel that at present, both governments and the private sector

03:20.240 --> 03:24.880
are quite happy to rely on neoliberalism and market forces

03:24.880 --> 03:28.560
and allow them to decide the direction of innovation.

03:28.560 --> 03:30.320
And this is extremely dangerous.

03:31.280 --> 03:33.680
How dangerous? Well, don't take it from me.

03:34.640 --> 03:37.520
Want to know where we could be heading as a species?

03:37.520 --> 03:39.600
Have a look at our friend Oliver here.

03:39.600 --> 03:43.440
Just a century ago, millions of horses in the UK could earn their keep

03:43.440 --> 03:47.360
in agriculture, in the mines, in industry, in transport.

03:48.400 --> 03:52.560
The fact is that for almost every job that horses once monopolised,

03:52.560 --> 03:55.360
today they're not worth their feed and stapling.

03:56.000 --> 03:58.720
They couldn't give their labour away at any price.

03:59.600 --> 04:03.600
We can only hope that the robots are a little more sympathetic to us

04:03.600 --> 04:05.200
than we were to the horses.

